## Why generics exist

Without generics you have two bad options for a reusable function: write it once per type, or use `any` and throw away checking. A **type parameter** lets one function stay type-safe for every type:

```ts
function identity<T>(value: T): T {
  return value;
}
const n = identity(42);       // T inferred as number → n: number
const s = identity('hi');     // T inferred as string → s: string
```

Line by line: `<T>` *declares* a type variable — a placeholder filled in at each call site. `value: T` says the parameter has that type, and `: T` says the return has the *same* one. At `identity(42)` the compiler infers `T = number`, so `n` is a `number` — no cast, no `any`.

### The progression you will build

```ts
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T | null {
  return arr.length ? arr[0] : null;
}
function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(items: T[], key: K): T[K][] {
  return items.map((item) => item[key]);
}
```

`first` relates an *array of T* to a *single T*. `pluck` (the end of this lesson's arc) relates two type parameters to each other — the key must belong to the item type.

### Common mistakes

- Reaching for `any` — it compiles, but the relationship between input and output is lost.
- Writing `<T>` and never using it twice. A type parameter earns its place by appearing in **two** positions (arg ↔ return, or two args).

## Telling the compiler what T can do

An unconstrained `T` could be *anything*, so you can do almost nothing with it — `value.length` is an error because a number has no `.length`. A **constraint** narrows the universe:

```ts
function longer<T extends { length: number }>(a: T, b: T): T {
  return a.length >= b.length ? a : b;
}
longer('hello', 'hi');     // ok — strings have length
longer([1, 2], [3]);       // ok — arrays have length
// longer(5, 10)           // error: number has no length
```

Line by line: `T extends { length: number }` reads "T can be any type **that at least has** a numeric length". Inside the body, `.length` is now legal; at the call site, anything without it is rejected at compile time.

### keyof — the keys of a type, as a type

`keyof { id: number; name: string }` is the union `'id' | 'name'`. Combined with a constraint it ties two parameters together:

```ts
function getProp<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}
getProp({ id: 1, name: 'Ada' }, 'name');   // string
// getProp({ id: 1 }, 'nope')             // error: not a key of the object
```

`K extends keyof T` means "K is one of T's property names", and the indexed-access type `T[K]` is "the type stored at that property". Misspell the key and the *compiler* catches it.

### Common mistakes

- Constraining when you don't need to — `identity` needs no constraint at all.
- `key: string` instead of `K extends keyof T`: it compiles the function but lets every caller pass garbage keys.

## Factories + closures = typed containers

A **factory function** returns an object whose methods close over private state. Add a type parameter and you get a container that works for any element type while staying fully typed:

```ts
function createStack<T>() {
  const items: T[] = [];            // private — nothing outside can touch it
  return {
    push(item: T) { items.push(item); },
    pop(): T | null { return items.length ? items.pop()! : null; },
    peek(): T | null { return items.length ? items[items.length - 1] : null; },
    size(): number { return items.length; },
  };
}
const s = createStack<number>();
s.push(1); s.push(2);
s.pop();          // 2 — stacks are LIFO: Last In, First Out
```

Line by line: `items` lives in the closure — callers can only reach it through the returned methods, so the invariants (LIFO order, accurate size) cannot be corrupted from outside. The explicit `createStack<number>()` pins `T` because there is no argument to infer from. `pop` and `peek` return `T | null` so the empty case is in the type, not a surprise `undefined`.

A **queue** is the same idea with FIFO order: `enqueue` pushes to the back, `dequeue` shifts from the front.

### Common mistakes

- Exposing the backing array (`return { items, ... }`) — now anyone can bypass your methods.
- Returning raw `undefined` from pop/peek instead of a deliberate `T | null`.
- Forgetting that each factory call makes an **independent** instance with its own state.

## One field that tells you everything

A **discriminated union** models "this value is one of several shapes" with a shared literal field — the *discriminant* — that identifies which shape you hold:

```ts
type Shape =
  | { kind: 'square'; size: number }
  | { kind: 'rect'; width: number; height: number }
  | { kind: 'triangle'; base: number; height: number };

function area(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case 'square':   return shape.size * shape.size;
    case 'rect':     return shape.width * shape.height;
    case 'triangle': return (shape.base * shape.height) / 2;
  }
}
```

Line by line: each union member carries `kind` with a *different string literal type*. Inside `case 'square'`, the compiler **narrows** `shape` to the square member, so `shape.size` is legal there and nowhere else. If you later add a `'circle'` member, every switch that does not handle it stops compiling (with `noImplicitReturns`, or an explicit `never` check) — the compiler finds all the code you forgot to update.

The same pattern types fallible operations:

```ts
type ApiResult<T> =
  | { ok: true; value: T }
  | { ok: false; error: string };
```

Checking `result.ok` narrows to the member that actually has `value` — you *cannot* touch `value` without checking first.

### Common mistakes

- Optional-field soup instead of a union: `{ kind: string; size?: number; width?: number; ... }` lets illegal combinations exist.
- Comparing the discriminant to a variable instead of a literal — narrowing only works with literal comparisons.

## States, actions, reducers

A **reducer** is a pure function `(state, action) => newState`. Model both sides as discriminated unions and illegal transitions become unrepresentable — or at least loudly visible:

```ts
type FetchState =
  | { status: 'idle' }
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: string }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };

type FetchAction =
  | { type: 'FETCH' }
  | { type: 'RESOLVE'; data: string }
  | { type: 'REJECT'; message: string };

function fetchReducer(state: FetchState, action: FetchAction): FetchState {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'FETCH':
      return state.status === 'idle' || state.status === 'error'
        ? { status: 'loading' } : state;
    case 'RESOLVE':
      return state.status === 'loading'
        ? { status: 'success', data: action.data } : state;
    case 'REJECT':
      return state.status === 'loading'
        ? { status: 'error', message: action.message } : state;
  }
}
```

Walk through RESOLVE: the action carries its payload (`data`), the guard says this transition is only legal *from loading*, and the return is a **brand-new state object** — reducers never mutate. An out-of-order action (RESOLVE while idle) returns the state unchanged: ignoring invalid transitions is the standard convention, and it is a *decision*, written down, not an accident.

Because the whole lifecycle is data in → data out, reducers are the most testable code in an app: feed a state and an action, assert the result. No mocks, no setup.

### Common mistakes

- Mutating: `state.data = ...; return state` — breaks change detection and time-travel debugging.
- Boolean soup (`isLoading`, `hasError`, `isDone`) instead of one status union — 2³ combinations, most meaningless.

## Types stop at the compiler; JSON doesn't care

Every type you have written so far is erased before the code runs. The JSON arriving from a request body, a file, or a third-party API has *whatever shape it has* — `as User` is a promise, not a check. The bridge between untyped input and typed code is a **runtime validator**:

```ts
type User = { id: number; name: string };

function isUser(obj: unknown): obj is User {
  return (
    typeof obj === 'object' && obj !== null &&
    typeof (obj as Record<string, unknown>).id === 'number' &&
    typeof (obj as Record<string, unknown>).name === 'string'
  );
}
```

Line by line: the parameter is `unknown` — the honest type for "we have no idea". The object check must come first (and must exclude `null`, which is `typeof 'object'`!). Each field check is a plain `typeof` comparison. The magic is the return type `obj is User`: when this returns true, the compiler narrows `obj` to `User` at the call site. **You wrote the proof; the compiler enforces its use.**

For richer feedback than true/false, return a result union instead:

```ts
function parseUser(obj: unknown): { ok: true; value: User } | { ok: false; error: string }
```

This is exactly what libraries like zod do — schemas, guards, and parse results. Hand-rolling small validators teaches you what those libraries automate.

### Common mistakes

- `typeof obj === 'object'` without the null check — `typeof null` is `'object'`.
- Trusting a cast: `JSON.parse(s) as User` validates nothing.
- Checking only the fields you remember — a validator is a *contract*, keep it next to the type.

## The three shapes of the same data

In a real backend one "user" exists in at least three shapes: the **database row** (`snake_case`, internal fields like `password_hash`), the **API DTO** (`camelCase`, safe fields only — DTO = Data Transfer Object), and sometimes a **request body** coming the other way. Keeping them as *separate types with explicit mappers* is what stops a password hash from leaking into a JSON response:

```ts
type UserRow = { user_id: number; full_name: string; password_hash: string; created_at: string };
type UserDto = { id: number; name: string; createdAt: string };

function toUserDto(row: UserRow): UserDto {
  return { id: row.user_id, name: row.full_name, createdAt: row.created_at };
}
```

Line by line: the mapper *constructs* the DTO — it never spreads the row, so new row columns (added by a future migration) stay private by default. Renames happen in exactly one place. The return type means forgetting a field is a compile error.

Case conversion is the mechanical half of this work:

```ts
function snakeToCamel(s: string): string {
  return s.replace(/_([a-z])/g, (_, ch) => ch.toUpperCase());
}
```

The regex finds each underscore-plus-letter; the replacer swaps the pair for the upper-cased letter: `created_at` → `createdAt`.

### Common mistakes

- `return { ...row }` in a mapper — today it works, next migration it leaks a column.
- Converting case at random layers. Pick the boundary (usually the DB adapter or the serializer) and convert exactly once.

